From d4da7d56bc833421f74c1616582dd8cc10f6d7b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <florian.stosse@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:28:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] do not modify `environ` when tracking the
 environment strings

When `main` takes `argc` and `argv`, `eacsl_memory_init` calls `argv_alloca`,
which also registers the environment strings. Both observation models walked
the strings with `while (*environ) { ...; environ++; }`, incrementing the
global `environ` itself. When the loop ended, `environ` pointed to the
terminating NULL entry: the instrumented program saw an empty environment,
and every later `getenv` returned NULL.

Walk the array with a local cursor, as `get_stack_start` in
`e_acsl_shadow_layout.c` already does. `environ` keeps its value. With
temporal analysis, the segment model still replaces each entry by its
shadow copy, as before.

The new test `libc/getenv.c` checks that `getenv("PATH")` is not NULL in a
`main(int argc, char **argv)`, with the segment model and with the bittree
model and full memory tracking. `temporal/t_getenv.c` did not catch the bug
because it accepts a NULL result.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
